**`PendSV`**: software-pended exception used here for task switching.

An infinite prime search never returns to a cooperative superloop, freezing LED, button and report work.

**Preemption** pauses a calculation for urgent work, then restores its exact state.

Cortex-M implements this with interrupts, PendSV and a separate task stack.

Implementation / numeric example:

```c
for (;;) {                               /* find primes, forever */
    uint32_t d = 3;
    while (d * d <= n && n % d != 0u) { d += 2u; }
    if (d * d > n) { primes++; }
    n += 2u;
}
```

**`TCB`**: task control block.
**`xPSR`**: combined program status register.
**`lr`**: link register / return address.
**`pc`**: program counter / instruction address.
**`sp`**: stack pointer.

A task's **context** lets it resume at the paused instruction: its stack, r0–r12, lr, pc, xPSR and saved stack pointer.

The TCB stores sp first, at byte offset zero, as assembly requires. The TCB also records name, current priority, base priority, state and wake deadline. Each task stack holds its paused registers; the TCB points to them.

Implementation / numeric example:

```c
typedef struct os_task {
    uint32_t   *sp;          /* MUST be first - the assembly reads [tcb + 0] */
    const char *name;
    uint8_t     prio, base_prio, state;
    uint32_t    wake;
    ...
} os_task_t;
```

**`round robin`**: taking turns among tasks of equal priority.

**`CPU`**: central processing unit.
**`NVIC`**: Nested Vectored Interrupt Controller.

Pick the highest-priority READY task.

Scan after current and replace a candidate only for strictly greater priority. Equal-priority tasks then take turns, one tick each.

Always-READY idle at priority zero guarantees a candidate.

Kernel priorities increase with urgency, whereas NVIC priority zero is most urgent: comparisons must specify which numbering scheme they use.

Implementation / numeric example:

```c
static os_task_t *pick(void)
{
    uint32_t cur = os_current ? (uint32_t)(os_current - tasks) : 0u;
    os_task_t *best = 0;
    for (uint32_t i = 1; i <= n_tasks; i++) {
        os_task_t *t = &tasks[(cur + i) % n_tasks];
        if (t->state == OS_READY && (best == 0 || t->prio > best->prio)) best = t;
    }
    return best;
}
```

**`ownership`**: rule determining which component may use or modify a resource.
**`critical section`**: short block protected from concurrent updates.

A **mutex** gives one task ownership of a resource. Only contenders wait; unrelated tasks and interrupts continue.

PRIMASK briefly protects kernel bookkeeping. A task unable to obtain a mutex becomes BLOCKED, records waiting_on and requests scheduling. It restores the previous interrupt mask on leaving the update and rechecks ownership when resumed.

Restoring the previous mask supports nesting. An ISR must finish without sleeping, even when a mutex exists.

Implementation / numeric example:

```c
void os_mutex_lock(os_mutex_t *m)
{
    uint32_t pm = enter();                       /* PRIMASK, as always   */
    while (m->owner != 0) {
        os_current->state      = OS_BLOCKED;     /* wait - off the CPU   */
        os_current->waiting_on = m;
        schedule(); leave(pm);  pm = enter();    /* switched out here    */
    }
    m->owner = os_current;
    leave(pm);
}
```

**`CPU`**: central processing unit.

The polled library output-adapter _write is weak. uart.c defines a strong compatible function, so linking replaces exactly that syscall while retaining other stubs. printf then returns once text enters the ring, before the final bit leaves the pin, though a full ring still waits.

This is the same weak-symbol mechanism as default vector handlers.

Callers remain independent of the driver implementation.

Implementation / numeric example:

```c
/* _lib/retarget.c */
__attribute__((weak)) int _write(int fd, const char *buf, int len) { ...polled... }

/* 08_UART_And_Python_Host/uart.c */
int _write(int fd, const char *buf, int len) { ...ring buffer... }
```

**`checksum`**: short value recomputed from message contents to detect some corruption.
**`formatter`**: code that converts values into output text.
**`checksum-invalid`**: not matching the checksum recomputed from the message.

Hold print_lock across the entire vprintf call to serialize complete lines and ring producers.

Otherwise telemetry and replies can mix characters into checksum-invalid frames. newlib-nano also has shared formatter state; character-level locking cannot protect concurrent formatting.

Renode's instantaneous transmit did not open the race window, so no observed corruption there is not proof of safety.

Implementation / numeric example:

```c
static void say(const char *fmt, ...)
{
    va_list ap;  va_start(ap, fmt);
    os_mutex_lock(&print_lock);
    vprintf(fmt, ap);
    os_mutex_unlock(&print_lock);
    va_end(ap);
}
```

**`SysTick`**: core countdown timer used for ticks.

Soft dog starts at boot with 1000 ms timeout and counts SysTick interrupts.

WWDG uses PCLK, armed by command, and Wokwi documents it as implemented but untested.

IWDG uses LSI and is not implemented there.

An interrupt-disabled hang freezes the soft timer, requiring hardware recovery.

SysTick can optionally feed under ISR policy before testing soft expiration.

On silicon independent IWDG is suitable. Simulator arming without observed reset is not proof of recovery.

Implementation / numeric example:

```c
void SysTick_Handler(void)
{
    ms_now++;
    if (wd_mode == WD_ISR) { dog_feed(); }
    if (dog_soft_tick()) {
        crash_rec.uptime_ms = ms_now;
        crash_reset(RW_SOFTDOG);
    }
}
```

Factual reference:

| Dog | Clock | Armed | In Wokwi | On silicon |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| **soft** | SysTick interrupt | at boot, 1000 ms | **bites** | bites, unless interrupts stop |
| **WWDG** | PCLK / 4096 / 2^WDGTB | `arm wwdg` | untested by Wokwi | bites; ≤ 699 ms |
| **IWDG** | LSI, its own oscillator | `arm iwdg` | **never bites** | bites — the one to ship |

**`PRIMASK`**: configurable-interrupt mask register.
**`VAL`**: SysTick current counter value.

Idle measurement reuses SysTick, counting down from 47999 once per millisecond.

Mask interrupts and check no work is due. Read VAL before and after WFI, account for wrap, and add elapsed time to idle before restoring PRIMASK. This excludes handler work.

On ARMv6-M, pending interrupts wake WFI under PRIMASK; handlers wait until unmasking. This protects the work-check/sleep transition from arriving interrupts. Four host elapsed-time checks include wrap cases.

CPU load in parts per thousand is 1000-idle_ms*1000/window. Spinning instead of sleeping changes electrical behavior even when useful work is similar. Physical current still needs a silicon measurement.

Implementation / numeric example:

```c
__disable_irq();
if (ms_now == t) {
    uint32_t v0 = SysTick->VAL;
    __WFI();                                    /* sleep: SLEEPDEEP = 0 */
    uint32_t v1 = SysTick->VAL;
    idle_cycles += systick_elapsed(v0, v1, SysTick->LOAD);
}
__enable_irq();                                 /* handlers run here */
```

**`ownership`**: rule determining which component may use or modify a resource.

**`CMSIS`**: Cortex Microcontroller Software Interface Standard.
**`HAL`**: hardware abstraction layer.

HAL GPIO and CMSIS ultimately emit the same peripheral store.

With assertions disabled, the compiled HAL path has eleven instructions versus four direct. Argument/call/test overhead is small.

Larger dependencies include handles/callbacks/init and HAL watchdog timeout's expectation of HAL_GetTick/SysTick ownership.

Read manufacturer HAL to learn key and wait sequences. Choose direct access where exact behavior and ownership matter.

This one build comparison cannot establish costs of every HAL operation.

Implementation / numeric example:

```c
void HAL_GPIO_WritePin(GPIO_TypeDef *GPIOx, uint16_t GPIO_Pin, GPIO_PinState PinState)
{
  /* Check the parameters */
  assert_param(IS_GPIO_PIN(GPIO_Pin));
  assert_param(IS_GPIO_PIN_ACTION(PinState));

  if (PinState != GPIO_PIN_RESET)
  {
    GPIOx->BSRR = (uint32_t)GPIO_Pin;
  }
  else
  {
    GPIOx->BRR = (uint32_t)GPIO_Pin;
  }
}
```

Factual reference:

| | instructions on the path | the store |
|---|---|---|
| `HAL_GPIO_WritePin(GPIOA, 1 << 5, GPIO_PIN_SET)` | 11 (args, `push`, `bl`, `cmp`, `beq`, `str`, `bx`, `pop`) | `str r1, [r0, #24]` |
| `GPIOA->BSRR = 1u << 5` | 4 | `str r2, [r3, #24]` |

Logical pixel x, y uses page y>>3 and bit y&7.

Storage adds 1 to x because byte 0 of each page is its I²C control prefix.

For logical column 40 on page 1, y9/y12 set bits 1/4 to 0x12.

Unsigned bounds tests clip negative and oversized coordinates. Shifts/masks avoid divide.

Mark dirty only when the stored byte actually changes, so repainting an already-lit pixel creates no bus traffic.

The source's fb[1][40] label is logical. Physical array indexing is [1][41].

Worked example: At (40,12), page is 1 and mask is 0x10. The prefix means the physical byte is fb[1][41], not fb[1][40].

Implementation / numeric example:

```c
void oled_pixel(int x, int y, int colour)
{
    if ((unsigned)x >= OLED_W || (unsigned)y >= OLED_H) { return; }
    uint8_t *b = &fb[y >> 3][1 + x];
    uint8_t  m = (uint8_t)(1u << (y & 7));
    uint8_t  old = *b;
    if (colour == OLED_ON)       { *b |= m; }
    else if (colour == OLED_XOR) { *b ^= m; }
    else                         { *b &= (uint8_t)~m; }
    if (*b != old) { dirty |= (uint8_t)(1u << (y >> 3)); }
}
```

Bit-field reference (inclusive positions):

| Register or encoding | Width / value | Fields |
|---|---|---|
| `fb[1][40] ; column 40, page 1 = rows 8..15` | 8 = 0x12 | 7 y15, 6 y14, 5 y13, 4 y12, 3 y11, 2 y10, 1 y9, 0 y8 |

**`WFI`**: wait-for-interrupt instruction.

Maintain next+=20 ms and perform every due update before one render/flush.

Read pad inside each update so one-call edges are consumed on their proper tick.

A 25 ms render causes a later pass to run two updates, preserving simulation cadence while reducing picture rate.

Sleep with WFI if nothing due.

Cap catch-up work at four, then reset next=now+20 to drop unrecoverable elapsed time rather than stall rendering.

Sound polling and profiler mode sit in the platform loop.

Implementation / numeric example:

```c
while ((int32_t)(ms - next) >= 0) {
    pad_read(&pad);
    if (pad.pressed & PAD_SEL) { full_repaint ^= 1; }
    arcade_step(&pad);
    updates++;
    next += TICK_MS;
    if (++ran == MAX_CATCHUP) {           /* hopelessly behind: give up */
        next = ms + TICK_MS;              /* the lost time, rather than */
        break;                            /* spiral into catching up    */
    }
}
if (ran == 0) {               /* nothing due: sleep until the next tick */
    __WFI();
    continue;
}
sound_poll(ms);               /* start any effect the update asked for */
arcade_draw();                /* RENDER: once, however many updates ran */
if (full_repaint) { oled_invalidate(); }
oled_flush();
```

**`XOR`**: exclusive OR, which keeps a bit when its inputs differ.

xorshift32 uses XOR shifts 13, 17, 5 and has period 2^32-1 for a nonzero seed. Zero remains stuck. rng_below scales the high sixteen bits using multiplication, avoiding M0+ divide, though bounded scaling can be biased.

Start-tick seeding permits variety and scripted replay: same seed/inputs gave 2934 identical ticks, score and framebuffer hash. A changed seed relocates apples.

Timing seeds can repeat and outputs are predictable, so this is not suitable for secrets.

AVR used 16-bit shifts 7/9/8 and period 65535.

Implementation / numeric example:

```c
uint32_t rng_next(void)
{
    uint32_t x = rng_state;
    x ^= x << 13;
    x ^= x >> 17;
    x ^= x << 5;
    return rng_state = x;
}
uint32_t rng_below(uint32_t n) { return ((rng_next() >> 16) * n) >> 16; }
```
